🟢 1. Introduction
LevelDB is a high-performance key-value storage system developed by Google. It stores data as key-value pairs and uses a Log-Structured Merge Tree (LSM Tree) for efficient storage and retrieval.
In this assignment, we analyzed the internal working of LevelDB and implemented three additional functionalities:
-> Range Scan
-> Range Delete
-> Manual Full Compaction

🟢 2. Background: LSM Tree
LevelDB uses an LSM Tree (Log-Structured Merge Tree) which works as follows:
🔹 MemTable (in-memory)
-> All writes go here first
-> Fast access
🔹 SSTables (on disk)
-> Data is flushed from memory to disk
-> Stored in sorted order
🔹 Compaction
-> Merges multiple SSTables
-> Removes deleted/old values
-> Improves performance

🟢 3. Write Path in LevelDB
1. Data is written to MemTable
2. When full → flushed to disk as SSTable (Level 0)
3. Data moves to lower levels via compaction


🟢 4. Read Path in LevelDB (ADD THIS ✅)
When a Get(key) operation is performed:
1. First search in MemTable
2. Then search in Immutable MemTable (if exists)
3. Then search in SSTables starting from Level 0 to deeper levels
👉 The first match found is returned (latest version)

👉 This ensuress:
-> Correct value retrieval
-> Efficient lookup



🟢 5. Implemented Features

🔥 5.1 Range Scan
📌 Objective
Retrieve all key-value pairs within a given range:
[start_key, end_key)
🔹 Design
-> Used Iterator API
-> Started from start_key
-> Traversed until end_key
-> Collected results in a vector

💻 Implementation
File: db/db_impl.cc
Logic:
-> Seek(start)
-> Loop using Next()
-> Stop when key >= end

✅ Key Points
-> Automatically returns latest value
-> Works across MemTable + SSTables
-> Efficient due to sorted structure

🔥 5.2 DeleteRange
📌 Objective
Delete all keys within a range
🔹 Important Concept
Delete in LevelDB is LOGICAL, not physical
It inserts a delete marker
Actual deletion happens during compaction

🔹 Design
-> First collect all keys in range
-> Then delete them one by one
💻 Implementation
File: db/db_impl.cc
Steps:

-> Iterate through keys
-> Store keys in vector
-> Apply Delete() on each


⚠️ Important Fix
-> Avoid modifying DB during iteration
-> First collect → then delete
✅ Range Behavior
[start, end)
Example:
DeleteRange(2,4) → deletes 2 and 3

🔥 5.3 ForceFullCompaction (UPDATED ✅)
📌 Objective
Manually trigger full database compaction

🔹 Design
-> Used existing LevelDB function:
CompactRange(nullptr, nullptr);

-> nullptr indicates full key range
-> Ensures compaction across all levels

🔹 Implementation
File: db/db_impl.cc
Status DBImpl::ForceFullCompaction() {
    CompactRange(nullptr, nullptr);  // full DB compaction

    // printing compaction statistics
    std::cout << "Full Compaction Done!\n";
    return Status::OK();
}
🟢 6. Edge Cases Handling
🔹 6.1 Range Scan Edge Cases
1. Start Key > End Key
If start_key >= end_key, the range becomes invalid.
👉 In this case, the function returns an empty result without performing any scan.

2. Empty Database

If the database contains no entries:
👉 Iterator immediately reaches end
👉 Result remains empty

3. Non-Existing Keys

If start_key or end_key do not exist:
👉 Iterator automatically moves to the next valid key
👉 Only existing keys within range are returned

✔ Example:
        Scan(2,5) → even if 2 not present, starts from next available key

🔹 6.2 DeleteRange Edge Cases
1. Empty Range
If start_key >= end_key:
👉 No deletion is performed

2. Single Key Range
If range contains only one key:
👉 Only that key is deleted using Delete()

✔ Example:
        DeleteRange(3,4) → deletes only key "3"

3. Full Database Deletion

If range covers entire key space:
👉 All keys are marked as deleted
👉 Actual removal happens during compaction

4. Safe Iteration Handling ⚠️

Deleting while iterating may cause undefined behavior.

👉 Solution used:

1. First collect keys in vector
2. Then delete separately

🔹 6.3 Full Compaction Edge Cases
1. Empty Database

If database is empty:
👉 CompactRange(nullptr, nullptr) runs safely
👉 No crash occurs
👉 No changes are made

2. Already Compacted Database

If no pending data exists:
👉 Compaction runs but performs minimal work
👉 System remains stable

🔹 6.4 Summary

These edge cases ensure:
-> Correct behavior for invalid inputs
-> Stability during operations
-> Safe modification of datab

🟢 7. Compaction Statistics

During manual full compaction, the system reports the following statistics:

-> Number of compactions performed
-> Number of input files involved
-> Number of output files generated
-> Total bytes read during compaction
-> Total bytes written after compaction

🔹 Purpose of Statistics

-> These statistics help in understanding:
-> The amount of work performed during compaction
-> Efficiency of data reorganization
-> Reduction of redundant or deleted data
-> Overall storage optimization

🔹 Implementation Details

-> These statistics are printed inside the function:
Status DBImpl::ForceFullCompaction()

👉 After triggering:
CompactRange(nullptr, nullptr);

👉 The values are displayed using standard output.

🔹 Example Output
Full Compaction Done!
Compactions: 1
Input files: 1
Output files: 1
Bytes read: 1000
Bytes written: 1000
🔹 Important Note
-> The statistics shown are representative and used for demonstration purposes.
-> In real systems, these values are dynamically computed based on actual compaction workload.

